#include \(dqserver.h\(dq

void session_cleanup(client_session_t *session);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Releases all resources associated with a client session.
Closes all open file descriptors owned by the session and resets fields
to safe values.
.PP
The cleanup sequence is:
.IP "1." 3
If \f[CR]write_fd\f[R] is open (\f[CR]>= 0\f[R]) and distinct from
\f[CR]fd\f[R], close \f[CR]write_fd\f[R] and set it to \f[CR]\-1\f[R].
.IP "2." 3
Close \f[CR]fd\f[R] if open (\f[CR]>= 0\f[R]) and set it to
\f[CR]\-1\f[R].
.IP "3." 3
Close both ends of \f[CR]stdout_pipe\f[R] if open (\f[CR]>= 0\f[R]).
.IP "4." 3
Close both ends of \f[CR]stderr_pipe\f[R] if open (\f[CR]>= 0\f[R]).
.PP
This function is safe to call multiple times on the same session.
After cleanup, the session structure should not be reused without
calling \f[CR]session_init()\f[R] again.
.SH PARAMETERS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]session\f[R] \(en Pointer to the \f[CR]client_session_t\f[R]
structure to clean up.
Must not be NULL.
The structure must have been previously initialized by
\f[CR]session_init()\f[R].
.SH RETURN VALUE
None.
.SH ERRORS
None.
Errors from \f[CR]close()\f[R] are silently ignored.
.SH SEE ALSO
\f[CR]session_init\f[R](3), \f[CR]session_handle\f[R](3)
.SH NOTES
Called automatically after \f[CR]session_handle()\f[R] returns in the
main server loop, and also on error paths during connection setup.
.PP
The function does not free the \f[CR]client_session_t\f[R] structure
itself, as it is typically stack\-allocated.
.PP
Care is taken to avoid double\-closing: \f[CR]write_fd\f[R] is only
closed if it differs from \f[CR]fd\f[R], preventing a double\-close when
both point to the same socket descriptor (the common case for Unix
domain socket transport).
